use console.println

/// Splits `text` by a separator its caller chose. Not the standard library: an
/// ordinary Cove function with a caller, between `main` and the operation that
/// refuses, so that the diagnostic below has a frame to blame that is neither
/// the entry point nor the machine.
fn pieces(text: String, separator: String) -> Array<String> {
  text.split(separator)
}

/// Pins `String.split`'s refusal of an empty separator, word for word.
///
/// An empty separator would match between every character rather than answer
/// the question the method asks, so the operation refuses it and says which
/// operation, which parameter, why, and what to reach for instead. The
/// `chars()` in the help is the operation that actually means "take this apart
/// character by character", and it is a real method with a corpus of its own.
///
/// This is the half of `String.split`'s contract that `values_string_split`
/// cannot hold, because a refusal stops the run and takes every row after it:
/// one case, one refusal. [ADR 0064](../../../docs/adr/0064-an-intrinsic-names-a-machine-not-a-method.md)'s
/// Decision 8 gates a migration on the diagnostic being unchanged, and this
/// file is where "unchanged" is written down.
///
/// **It is what held the move to its word**, and the move happened: issue
/// [#461](https://github.com/myuon/cove/issues/461) gave a Cove body
/// `core.refuse` (ADR 0067), and `std.string.split` raises this refusal
/// itself. The message, the rule, the help and the blamed line in `pieces` are
/// the bytes the `Intrinsic::StringSplit` arm wrote; what the move added is one
/// context block, the `std/string.cove` line that raises, labelled `in the
/// standard library` — ADR 0058's rule, the one
/// `tests/e2e/fail_stringbuilder_byte_range/expected.err` and `Int.abs`
/// already follow.
export fn main() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let parts = pieces("a,b,c", "")
  println("{parts.length()}")?
  Ok(())
}
